## Migration Analysis

**APIs found that break on .NET 8:**

| API | Line | Pattern |
|-----|------|---------|
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Stop()` | Blocking call interruption (also obsolete: `SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9) |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `Stop()` | Cancellable work loop fallback |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | `RunLoop()` | Blocking call — not cancellation-aware |

**Classification:** This combines a **cancellable work loop** with **blocking call interruption**. The `Stop()` method tries `Interrupt` then falls back to `Abort` — both throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 8.

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var token = _cts.Token;
        var handles = new[] { _newEvent.WaitHandle, token.WaitHandle };

        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // WaitAny wakes on either a new event OR cancellation
            WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();           // signals the token → WaitAny wakes up
        _thread?.Join(5000);     // wait for graceful exit
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

## Key Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` / `Thread.Interrupt()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — cooperative cancellation replaces forced termination.
2. **`ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` → `WaitHandle.WaitAny(event, token.WaitHandle)`** — the blocked thread now wakes on *either* a new item or a cancellation signal.
3. **`while (true)` → `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)`** — the loop checks the token each iteration.
4. **`ManualResetEvent` → `ManualResetEventSlim`** — lighter-weight; suitable for in-process signaling.
5. **Guard the `Dequeue`** — a spurious wake (cancellation) could hit an empty queue; the original code would throw `InvalidOperationException`.

---

## Pitfalls to Watch For

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **`WaitOne()` without the token** | If you keep bare `_newEvent.WaitOne()`, cancellation can't wake the thread — `Stop()` hangs forever. Always use `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle`. |
| **Not guarding `Dequeue`** | The original code assumed every wake = an item. With two wake sources (event + cancellation), the queue may be empty. Always check `_queue.Count > 0`. |
| **Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** | `CTS` is `IDisposable` — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. Dispose in `Stop()`. |
| **Expecting immediate stop** | Cooperative cancellation only works at checkpoints. If `Handle(item)` runs for 30 seconds, cancellation waits until it returns. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `Handle` if it does long work. |
| **Race between multiple items** | The original code had a bug: multiple rapid `Enqueue` calls could signal `Set()` but only one `Dequeue` runs per wake. Consider draining the queue in a loop per wake cycle. |

### Optional: Drain-all-items fix

```csharp
// Inside RunLoop, after WaitAny returns and cancellation check passes:
_newEvent.Reset();
while (true)
{
    string? item;
    lock (_lock)
    {
        if (_queue.Count == 0) break;
        item = _queue.Dequeue();
    }
    Handle(item);
}
```

This ensures all queued items are processed per signal, fixing the pre-existing race in the original code.
